    {
      "id": "1409",
      "type": "related_content",
      "attributes": {
        "id": 1409,
        "relationship_type": "synonym",
        "body": "The imperative or command form  (命令形) gives strong, direct commands, e.g. 食べろ ('Eat!'). On its own, it sounds harsh, so it's often softened. なさい is a more polite alternative, often used by parents or teachers toward children, e.g. 野菜を食べなさい ('Eat your vegetables!'). Both forms command action but differ in tone.",
        "first_relatable": {
          "id": 1032,
